      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 3 \\cos{(x )}=- 4 y \\sin{(x )} + 1\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} - 3 \\cos{(x )}&=- 4 y \\sin{(x )} + 1 \\\\ 2 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 4 y \\sin{(x )}&=3 \\cos{(x )} + 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(2 \\sin{(x )})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{\\frac{3 \\cos{(x )}}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2 \\sin{(x )}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{3 \\cos{(x )}}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}.$",
