      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{2x-2 y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}&=\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2 y}{x}&=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\frac{2}{x})}_{P(x)}y&=\\underbrace{2}_{Q(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$",
